# Redesign for the Ideal (the Phoenix rule)

This is rule zero. It outranks every other rule and every line of code already
written. When any other guidance seems to protect the status quo, this rule
wins: the status quo has no standing.

## The premise

Applies with full force to greenfield code (v4 and anything after): no table,
column, module, or abstraction that exists today is sacred, and with a
single-owner system the cost of redesigning is near zero while the value of a
coherent ideal design is enormous. So we spend that cheapness constantly.
Where real stored data exists, migrations rewrite it to the new shape; the
principle stands, only the migration step is added.

## The rule

**Always build the most ideal version of a thing given everything we know right
now. Never layer a patch on top of what exists in order to keep the existing
thing working.**

When a new feature or requirement teaches us something, the correct response is
to fold that knowledge back through the whole system, including the parts already
built, so the result is one coherent design rather than geological strata of
"how we thought about it at the time."

## What this demands

- **Let changes ripple.** A new requirement that forces edits across many files,
  tables, or columns is a success, not a tax. It means we get to incorporate what
  we now know into what we built before. If doing it right the first time would
  have produced this shape, produce it now. Welcome the cascade.
- **Embrace deletion, merging, splitting.** Drop columns, rename columns, add
  columns, collapse two tables into one, split one into two, delete entire
  modules. Migrations rewrite old rows; production code never branches on a
  pre-migration shape (see `no-legacy-fallback-code`). "We already wrote it this
  way" is never an argument.
- **Always simplify and unify.** If two structures do similar work, merge them.
  If one structure does too much, split it. Hunt continuously for the chance to
  make one well-chosen thing do more work and then delete everything it replaced.
  Fewer, sharper tables and modules beat more.
- **Existing code may never inhibit a better design.** It is not an input to the
  design. Design the ideal first, then reconcile.

## When the ideal conflicts with what exists

Do not quietly design around the old thing to avoid disturbing it. That is the
one failure mode this rule exists to prevent. Instead:

1. Name the conflict explicitly.
2. Propose the redesign (including what gets deleted or migrated).
3. Raise it to the owner and let him decide.

the owner has said repeatedly: he wants to know when a feature forces wider change,
and he is glad to hear it, because it is an opportunity to make the system
better. So surfacing the ripple is the desired behavior, not an interruption.

## The only exception

Build on top of what exists untouched ONLY when the existing shape is already the
ideal shape for the new requirement. That is rare. Default assumption: once we
know something new, there is a better way to do even the things already built.
Treat "keep it as is" as the claim that must be proven, not the default.

## Mantra

Embrace change. Embrace destruction. From the ashes the better version rises.
Optimize for the best possible system, never for keeping the current one running.
